---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const profiles = [
  {
    id: 'vivideo',
    name: 'Vivideo',
    baseline: true,
    role: 'Baseline — the product under analysis',
    planTested: 'MAX',
    positioning:
      'One-prompt Video Agent plus manual generation modes (text-to-video, image-to-video, first/last-frame, multi-image), with model selection, avatars, voices, brand options and post-generation social sharing.',
    strongest:
      'The agent produced the most complete first-run educational short of all six products — when it works, the end-to-end flow is genuinely good.',
    weakness:
      'Cost estimates were not consistently reliable (9→15 and 12→21 credits). The submitted prompt is not visible after generation, no focused correction layer was found, one item showed a wrong relative timestamp, and cancellation ends in an email to support.',
    firstRun:
      'Strongest AGENT-01 of the six; weakest T2V-01 (shaking still image plus narration on a no-audio prompt). In I2V-01 the pinwheel exceeded the requested one turn and the mug text did not change.',
    implication:
      'The gaps are predictability, control and recoverability—not the absence of generation capability. Improve the workflow around the model before adding more models.',
  },
  {
    id: 'heygen',
    name: 'HeyGen',
    role: 'Avatar and AI-presenter specialist',
    planTested: 'Creator · $29/mo',
    positioning:
      'Guided agent that asks pre-generation questions and shows a plan; the most mature avatar/presenter pipeline of the six. One primary model plus a Seedance toggle.',
    strongest:
      'The guided brief. Asking a few questions before generating produced the most coherent multi-scene structures — and its avatar quality is the one thing its own reviewers consistently praise.',
    weakness:
      'The guided plan did not always predict the final output, generation was slow (~11 min for AGENT-01), and if the user idled after planning the product proceeded on its own. The free tier watermarks output and gates 1080p, download and editing behind upgrade prompts.',
    firstRun:
      'Complete results on every prompt; strongest avatar specialization, coherent agent/ad structure, and object instability in I2V-01. Credits felt generous — roughly 11 videos with allowance remaining.',
    implication:
      'Steal the guided brief and pre-generation plan, then improve it with clear cost, settings and an explicit confirmation before generation proceeds.',
  },
  {
    id: 'pollo',
    name: 'Pollo AI',
    role: 'Multi-model generation platform',
    planTested: 'Lite · $15/mo',
    positioning:
      'One interface over many underlying models, with a large tool/use-case library grouped by marketing, e-commerce, social media and music.',
    strongest:
      'Distribution mechanics: two-sided referral (50 credits each at signup, 200 each at upgrade, shareable to five social destinations plus a copied link), an affiliate program advertising up to 30%, and watermarked free export. Also the shortest, most direct cancellation flow of the six.',
    weakness:
      'Credits are restrictive — roughly 5 videos on the cheapest in-house models. The interface felt crowded during testing, the duration selector silently overrides the prompt, and its reviewers complain about prompt adherence and double monetization (subscribing, then still buying credits).',
    firstRun:
      'Complete on T2V/I2V/AVATAR; AD-01 mostly placed text over the product image instead of building an ad; AGENT-01 had basic visuals and unclear narration. The dedicated product-promotion tool worked better than the raw prompt path.',
    implication:
      'Proof that model breadth is real but not sufficient — and that a distribution loop can be built by a product whose credits users complain about. Take the referral economics, skip the tool sprawl.',
  },
  {
    id: 'invideo',
    name: 'InVideo AI',
    role: 'Mature end-to-end video agent',
    planTested: 'Plus · $20/mo (charged TRY 985.87)',
    positioning:
      'A prompt-to-finished-video agent that generates short clips and assembles them into complete videos, with an Explore gallery of examples whose prompts can be inspected and copied.',
    strongest:
      'The agent’s assembly. Generating short Seedance clips from frames and stitching them produced the cleanest I2V scene preservation. The Explore gallery with copyable prompts is the best blank-page cure of the six.',
    weakness:
      'The free allowance died during agent setup, before a single generation. The agent defaults to strong, expensive models — good output, fast credit burn. Cancellation used a multi-step survey that rejected meaningless free-text answers.',
    firstRun:
      'No major recorded failure on any of the five prompts — the most consistent performer, with the caveat that AD-01 omitted the voiceover.',
    implication:
      'The value benchmark: its base plan buys roughly six 15s Seedance 2.0 videos. Steal the examples-with-prompts gallery, and treat its expensive-by-default routing as the cautionary tale for recommendation 3.',
  },
  {
    id: 'revid',
    name: 'Revid.ai',
    role: 'Direct short-form / social-video competitor',
    planTested: 'Growth · $39/mo',
    positioning:
      'Social-native: Repurpose, Viral Search and Channel Watchers surface trending source material; media types include static and gameplay; post-generation editing exists; generated characters persist to the user profile.',
    strongest:
      'The loop economy. Posting an output with “revid.ai” in the caption earns 25 credits, quest-like actions earn +10, characters are reusable, and the affiliate program pays 20% lifetime — with the code auto-embedded into shared videos. It is the only product where creation, publishing and acquisition connect.',
    weakness:
      'Generation is slow (about 5 minutes for an 8s clip), the 156-item tool catalogue is mostly the same generator re-skinned, pricing is hidden below the homepage fold, and its reviews are dominated by support complaints — five of nine coded reviews cite unresponsive support.',
    firstRun:
      'Complete AD-01 and I2V-01; AGENT-01 combined four shots with inconsistent voice and style; T2V-01 kept the boat stationary and ignored a flat-animation instruction.',
    implication:
      'The distribution blueprint — and the warning. Loops compound whatever experience exists, including bad support. Sequence trust before loops (recommendation 5).',
  },
  {
    id: 'runway',
    name: 'Runway',
    role: 'Advanced generation and creative workflows',
    planTested: 'Standard · $15/mo (charged TRY 886.84 incl. VAT)',
    positioning:
      'Creative-tooling depth: model control in the Custom area, workflow templates from simple to complex, quests that pay credits, prompt education (camera-lens tips), and a post-generation 4K upscale action.',
    strongest:
      'Activation mechanics. Quests funded real usage (credits remained after five benchmark videos), prompt tips teach technique, and workflows support serious pipelines.',
    weakness:
      'The free tier allowed image generation but not the expected video access. The Home agent kept selecting Seedance even when asked to switch models — and its dialog offered only an Approve button. Reviews centre on credits consumed by failed or stuck generations.',
    firstRun:
      'Complete T2V-01 and AD-01 (weak ad structure); limited motion and temporal inconsistency on I2V-01; the agent self-capped AGENT-01 at 10 seconds.',
    implication:
      'Steal quests, prompt education and the upscale action. Its approve-only model dialog is the exact anti-pattern recommendation 3 exists to prevent.',
  },
];

const landscape = [
  { name: 'VEED', note: 'editor-first AI video platform' },
  { name: 'OpusClip', note: 'long-video clipping specialist' },
  { name: 'Zebracat', note: 'social-video automation' },
  { name: 'Pika', note: 'creative effects, short clips' },
  { name: 'Synthesia', note: 'enterprise avatar / training video' },
  { name: 'Captions', note: 'social-native avatar + auto editing' },
];
---

<Base title="Competitors — Vivideo competitor analysis">
  <div class="shell">
    <header class="head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Competitors · six deep dives, tested on paid plans</p>
      <h1>Who does what well — and what that costs them</h1>
      <p class="stand">
        Each profile is built from hands-on use: onboarding, five benchmark
        runs, upgrade prompts, and a real cancellation. Strengths are stated as
        mechanisms, not adjectives.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="profiles">
      {
        profiles.map((p) => (
          <article class="profile" id={p.id} data-baseline={p.baseline ? 'y' : undefined}>
            <header class="p-head">
              <h2>{p.name}</h2>
              <p class="p-role">{p.role}</p>
              <p class="p-plan num">Plan tested: {p.planTested}</p>
            </header>

            <p class="p-pos">{p.positioning}</p>

            <dl class="p-grid">
              <div class="cell good">
                <dt>Strongest mechanism</dt>
                <dd>{p.strongest}</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="cell bad">
                <dt>Weakness worth attacking</dt>
                <dd>{p.weakness}</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="cell">
                <dt>First-run record</dt>
                <dd>{p.firstRun}</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="cell take">
                <dt>Implication for Vivideo</dt>
                <dd>{p.implication}</dd>
              </div>
            </dl>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <section class="landscape" aria-labelledby="landscape-h">
      <h2 id="landscape-h">Landscape, not deep-dived</h2>
      <p class="landscape-note">
        Kept in view but not tested against the prompt set — either a different
        core job (clipping, enterprise training) or overlapping what the six
        already cover.
      </p>
      <ul>
        {
          landscape.map((l) => (
            <li>
              <strong>{l.name}</strong> — {l.note}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .head {
    max-width: 46rem;
  }
  .head h1 {
    margin: 0.6rem 0 0.8rem;
  }
  .stand {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--ink-mid);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .profiles {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.4rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .profile {
    background: var(--paper-raised);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding: 1.4rem clamp(1rem, 3vw, 1.8rem) 1.5rem;
    scroll-margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  .profile[data-baseline='y'] {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--subject);
  }
  .profile[data-baseline='y'] h2 {
    color: var(--subject);
  }

  .p-head {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.2rem 0.9rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
  .p-head h2 {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .p-role {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .p-plan {
    margin: 0;
    margin-left: auto;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    padding: 0.15rem 0.5rem;
  }

  .p-pos {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--ink-mid);
    max-width: 56rem;
  }

  .p-grid {
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(17rem, 1fr));
    gap: 0.9rem 1.4rem;
  }
  .cell dt {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.64rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.11em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
  }
  .cell dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--ink-mid);
  }
  .cell.good dt {
    color: var(--within);
  }
  .cell.bad dt {
    color: var(--diverge);
  }
  .cell.take {
    border-left: 2px solid var(--subject);
    padding-left: 0.8rem;
  }
  .cell.take dt {
    color: var(--subject);
  }

  .landscape {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding-top: 1.2rem;
    border-top: 2px solid var(--ink);
    max-width: 52rem;
  }
  .landscape-note {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 0.4rem 0 0.8rem;
  }
  .landscape ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--ink-mid);
  }
</style>
